INV-sarum. `_taint_blind_reason` runs two checks that have to agree about what
"present in this repo" means, and they did not:

  1. the language census, built from behavior-map NODES -- decides which
     languages count as SUPPORTED;
  2. compute_boundary_coverage, which walks production-scoped EDGES -- asks
     whether each supported language actually produced call edges.

The 08-13 fix (adfaaeebf2) scoped the EDGE list so both checks walked one
population, and left the NODE census unscoped. So a language present only as
test fixtures was counted PRESENT by check 1 while its edges were deliberately
excluded by check 2, and the pair read as analyzer blindness: "supported
language(s) ... were analyzed but produced no call edges, so their I/O is
invisible". On a 152,154-edge self-survey that was elixir, go, java, rust and
swift -- every tracked file of each living under a test or fixture directory.

INV-motos' shape for the THIRD time inside this one function: sharing the
predicate is not enough when the callers run it over different populations.
Share the POPULATION.

THE DIRECTION WAS DECIDED, NOT GUESSED. This item was filed with its fork
stated rather than a remedy, because "should a fixture count?" is a
threat-model question and not a code one. SECURITY.md's scope statement
(WI-kozos, merged ae67059b51) answers it: the audit covers the INSTALLED CLI,
so a fixture is not part of what the claims describe. Production-scoping the
census is therefore parity with WI-bifob's flow filter, and
--include-non-production-sources widens BOTH so the two cannot disagree in the
other direction either.

THE OBVIOUS NARROWING WOULD HAVE BEEN WRONG, and this is the part worth
reading. "Keep only languages that produced scoped call edges" is simpler,
passes the repro, and destroys the check: it also drops a language that IS
production code and emits nothing -- which is exactly Kotlin, missing roughly
95% of its catalogued sinks. That is the F69.A1 blindness signal, and
suppressing it would turn "I cannot see this language" into a silent pass. The
scoping is therefore by PRESENCE IN PRODUCTION SOURCE, never by having
produced edges, and
test_a_PRODUCTION_language_that_emits_nothing_STILL_blocks pins it. That test
matters more than the repro test.

A CONTROL THAT PASSED FOR THE WRONG REASON, caught and recorded rather than
shipped: my first version of both integration tests passed `catalogs={}`, which
trips a DIFFERENT gate ("made calls but have no I/O catalog") before the census
question is ever reached. Both tests would have been green while testing
nothing. They now load real catalogues.

SCOPE, stated because the diff looks bigger than its effect: `languages`
(unscoped) remains the census for the BOUNDARY arm and the catalog-loading
loop, which answer a different question -- what must be loaded to classify
every edge in the map, fixtures included. Only the taint arm's census moves.
Boundary claim verdicts are untouched.

A FIRST VERSION OF THIS FIX WAS WRONG, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHAT CAUGHT IT.
Scoping the census by "does this node's path classify as a test" left the five
languages the item was filed about STILL in the census -- on the real
self-survey go, java, rust, swift and elixir survived on 9, 9, 8, 4 and 1
`<external>` nodes with zero production source files between them. An
`<external>` node is a reference TARGET minted because some other file named
it; reading it as evidence that the language is present in shipped code
inverts its meaning. Presence now requires a real source file
(`paths.names_no_source_file`, promoted from the private copy
`sketch._map_source_paths` has carried since INV-jumim -- the same question,
already asked once, in a second home).

One of my own tests had encoded the wrong rule and asserted a path-less node
counts as production. Corrected in place with the measurement that refutes it,
rather than deleted: TESTS ENCODE THE DEFECT THEY GUARD.

MEASURED on the real self-survey (43,488 nodes / 152,565 edges), bash peeled so
layer 2 is the front blocker, using production's own supported-language filter:

  before   "supported language(s) elixir, go, java, rust, swift were analyzed
            but produced no call edges, so their I/O is invisible"
  after    that blocker is GONE; the front blocker becomes layer 3 --
           "the analysis launches an external program at 81 call site(s)"

That is the four-layer stack draining as filed: census incoherence removed,
opaque launches now front, and layer 3's answer already shipped as the
`opaque_boundary` caveat kind (rc 3). Census: 24 languages -> 11.

